22:42:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a very clever way to look at it, and you're right to spot that loophole. Let me answer that directly. **In theory, yes—with infinite money, a studio could keep paying to renew the license forever.** **In practice, no—because the song owner can always say "no" or demand an impossible price.** Here's the catch you're missing: **The song owner is not obligated to renew the license at any price.** Let me give you a real-world example of why "just keep paying" doesn't work. **The "Happy Birthday" Song Case** For decades, the song "Happy Birthday to You" was owned by a company called Warner/Chappell. They charged a license fee every time the song was used in a movie or TV show. Studios paid it, year after year. Then, in 2015, a court ruled that Warner/Chappell didn't actually own the copyright. The song was ruled to be in the public domain. Suddenly, studios didn't have to pay anymore. But imagine if Warner/Chappell *had* owned it. They could have said to a studio: > "We know you've been paying us $10,000 a year for 20 years to use this song in your movie. Next year, the price is $10 million. Pay it or remove the song." The studio would have no choice. They couldn't say, "But we've been paying for 20 years!" The owner can raise the price to anything they want, or simply refuse to renew. **Another example: The Beatles** The Beatles are famously protective of their music. They have refused to license their songs for many movies and TV shows, even when offered huge sums of money. If a studio had a Beatles song in a movie and the license expired, the Beatles could simply say, "No, you cannot renew. We don't want our song in your movie anymore." No amount of money would change their mind. **So, to answer your question directly:** > "With enough money, can they make the rights permanent?" **No, because "permanent" requires the song owner to agree to a contract that says "this license never expires."** Most song owners will never sign that contract, because it gives away all their future control and income. **What the studio can do is keep renewing a temporary license, year after year, as long as the song owner agrees to keep renting it out.** But that's not "permanent." That's "indefinite, but revocable at any time." **The key difference:** - **Permanent:** The studio owns the right forever. The song owner cannot take it back. - **Indefinite rental:** The studio keeps paying, and the song owner keeps allowing it. But the song owner can stop allowing it at any time, for any reason, or for no reason at all. **So, the answer is:** No, a studio cannot simply "buy permanence" with enough money, because the song owner has the ultimate power to say "no" or to demand a price so high that it's cheaper to replace the song. The rights are never truly "secured forever." They are only secured for as long as the current contract lasts.
